WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.600
Staying compliant: annual returns, honest labels, and records.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.660
This is the video for module three of Business Registration and Compliance in Nigeria.

3
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:25.610
Registration is a one-time event. Compliance is a habit. An annual return tells the CAC, once a year: we are still here, and this is who we are. It is not the same as a tax return.

4
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:33.190
A company files every year, and the first is due not later than 18 months after incorporation.

5
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.990
Business names file every year too. Check the portal for the current due date.

6
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:50.820
Late filing brings penalties for each year missed. A business left too long can be struck off, and then it cannot open accounts, bid for contracts or apply to NAFDAC.

7
00:00:52.070 --> 00:01:06.280
For example, Grace registered a company in Port Harcourt over three years ago and never filed. A hotel wants to give her a uniform contract and asks for her status report. The public search shows she is not up to date.

8
00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:13.780
She logs into the portal with the email used at registration and sees the returns outstanding.

9
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:20.470
She prepares simple financial statements for each year from her bank statements and sales book.

10
00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.050
She files in order and pays the fees and penalties shown on the portal.

11
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:33.960
She keeps the contract, but pays penalties she could have avoided. Now a yearly phone reminder protects her.

12
00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:42.780
Report every change promptly. If the register still shows a director who has left, she may still be able to sign for the company.

13
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:54.970
Packaged food, drinks, cosmetics and chemicals need NAFDAC registration. Other manufactured goods may need SON. A service only needs CAC and tax.

14
00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:05.010
For example, Ifeyinwa in Owerri sells pepper sauce with a sticker that says NAFDAC approved and boosts immunity. She has not applied to NAFDAC yet.

15
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:18.330
She removes NAFDAC approved at once, and the health claim she cannot prove. She adds ingredients, the net content, her business name and address, and space for the NAFDAC number, batch and dates.

16
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.350
Never print a number you have not been given. Products with false claims can be seized.

17
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:38.020
The Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 protects names, phone numbers and payment details. Collect only what you need, tell customers why, and never share their details without consent.

18
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:47.590
If a customer reports a problem with a food or cosmetic, record the batch number and stop selling that batch until you understand it.

19
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.840
And as a woman owner, keep the business in your own name, keep your own login, and be a signatory on the bank account.

20
00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:07.820
Keep four records: sales, expenses, receipts and bank statements. A notebook or free phone spreadsheet is enough, if you use it every day. Here is what that gives you.

21
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.950
Rukayat's sales in March were 420,000 naira.

22
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.490
Flour, sugar, butter, eggs and packaging cost 231,000.

23
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:29.960
So gross profit is 189,000. Divided by 420,000, that is a 45 percent gross margin.

24
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.560
Gas, transport, data and her stall levy cost 64,000.

25
00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:47.780
Net profit: 125,000 naira. Her transfers and POS match her bank statement, so her records are complete enough to show a tax office or a bank.

26
00:03:49.030 --> 00:04:02.470
Nigeria's new tax laws took effect on 1 January 2026, so guidance is still developing. A company files company income tax returns with the Nigeria Revenue Service.

27
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:11.570
A company that meets the legal definition of a small company pays 0 percent company income tax, but it must still file.

28
00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:19.260
A business name owner pays personal income tax on profit through her State Internal Revenue Service.

29
00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:30.640
Companies must keep accounting records for six years. Even when tax due is nothing, filing proves you are compliant and helps you get a tax clearance certificate.

30
00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:43.840
To remember. File your annual return every year, and report changes promptly. Keep labels honest. Keep four simple records and back them up. And even when tax is zero, file.

31
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:52.260
Now run a status check on your business, audit one product label and customer list, and make your first monthly summary.

32
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.550
Continue with the lessons below.
